<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jerome</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 347–420</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Saint Jerome, Eusebius Hieronymus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jerome was a Christian priest, scholar and Doctor of the Church, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, who spent his later life as a monk in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> translation of the Bible, later known as the Vulgate, became the standard text of the Western Church for over a thousand years. He also wrote biblical commentaries, letters and a continuation of Eusebius' chronicle.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jerome.jpg" alt="Jerome" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Matthias Stom · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MatthiasStom-SaintJerome-Nantes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
